WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.570 --> 00:00:05.640
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann, and
today I'm going to talk to you

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:08.130
about how I learned Russian.

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:12.090
Remember if you enjoy these
videos, please subscribe, click

00:00:12.090 --> 00:00:14.130
on the bell to get notifications.

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:20.340
So I was about 60 and my approach
to language learning had always been

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:21.960
that you shouldn't focus on grammar.

00:00:22.485 --> 00:00:26.415
Uh, you need to get used to the language
through lots of listening and reading.

00:00:26.415 --> 00:00:28.485
I had done that with
Chinese, with Japanese.

00:00:28.634 --> 00:00:32.085
You need to get used to the patterns
of language and not focus on the

00:00:32.085 --> 00:00:34.635
itsy bitsy details of the language.

00:00:35.385 --> 00:00:38.504
And people said to me, that will
not work with Russian because

00:00:38.504 --> 00:00:40.905
Russian has very complex grammar.

00:00:41.265 --> 00:00:43.364
So that became a bit of a challenge.

00:00:43.995 --> 00:00:46.105
Uh, and also I had always enjoyed,

00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:51.460
you know, reading novels of Russian
literature in English when I was young.

00:00:51.730 --> 00:00:55.780
So I thought it would be cool
to be able to read uh Tolstoy

00:00:55.780 --> 00:00:57.490
or Dostoevsky in Russian.

00:00:57.519 --> 00:01:00.610
So with that, I started learning Russian.

00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:06.220
I began at that time, LingQ was very
much in a sort of rudimentary stage.

00:01:06.250 --> 00:01:10.340
It would take us three, four, five
seconds to save an individual LingQ.

00:01:10.905 --> 00:01:15.494
But I started obviously, you know, not
having a full fledged program at LingQ.

00:01:15.585 --> 00:01:21.794
I bought books, uh, Colloquial, uh,
Teach Yourself, Living Language,

00:01:21.794 --> 00:01:23.685
listened to those, read them.

00:01:23.865 --> 00:01:27.975
As soon as we had certain things that I
could do on LingQ, I started doing them.

00:01:28.285 --> 00:01:31.134
Yevgeny, who is a great
supporter of LingQ in

00:01:31.164 --> 00:01:34.065
petersburg, created a
lot of great content.

00:01:34.514 --> 00:01:40.244
Uh, as soon as I could I started
into, uh, you know, audio books.

00:01:40.274 --> 00:01:43.005
And I'm going to give you a brief view.

00:01:43.095 --> 00:01:46.365
In fact, if you look behind
me, you'll see, I stacked

00:01:46.365 --> 00:01:47.774
them up to have a look here.

00:01:47.774 --> 00:01:52.265
You'll see some of the, uh, DVDs,
some of the videos that I watched,

00:01:52.265 --> 00:01:54.315
some of the CDs that I listened to.

00:01:54.645 --> 00:01:58.815
The DVDs initially was more to,
to stimulate me and encourage me.

00:01:59.384 --> 00:02:00.854
The CDs was more for listening.

00:02:00.854 --> 00:02:02.324
I did a lot of listening.

00:02:02.835 --> 00:02:08.685
Uh, in those days, uh, we weren't yet able
to import, you know, from YouTube and so

00:02:08.685 --> 00:02:13.545
forth, but we were able to import some of
the classics, just the text, into LingQ.

00:02:14.025 --> 00:02:17.055
And then I would listen to the audio.

00:02:17.385 --> 00:02:22.875
And in fact, I'll give you a brief sort
of view of my profile with Russian.

00:02:22.875 --> 00:02:26.595
And you'll see that I began
in 2007 more or less, and that

00:02:26.595 --> 00:02:31.200
I've had a period of significant
activity, which then tapered off.

00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:32.520
So first have a look at that.

00:02:32.910 --> 00:02:37.940
I'm looking at my profile, and I get to
my profile by the way, by clicking on my,

00:02:39.410 --> 00:02:42.350
uh, image here and then going to profile.

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:47.090
And you'll see that I started
learning Russian in 2007.

00:02:47.660 --> 00:02:49.070
That's a long time ago.

00:02:49.070 --> 00:02:52.700
This is my hours of listening,
not accurate because most of my

00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:57.840
listening through this period
has been to CDs, watching movies,

00:02:57.870 --> 00:03:03.330
listening to Ekho Moskvy, and I'll
get into more detail on what I did.

00:03:03.330 --> 00:03:05.440
If I just look at my activities on LingQ.

00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:09.450
If I look  at my LingQs created,
you'll see that I've been active

00:03:09.450 --> 00:03:11.190
over a long period of time.

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:14.640
Even recently, I go in
occasionally and read an article

00:03:15.195 --> 00:03:17.755
on LingQ and create some LingQs.

00:03:17.775 --> 00:03:22.275
So my known words total
has grown in this fashion.

00:03:22.275 --> 00:03:23.025
I don't know...

00:03:23.115 --> 00:03:26.295
when we were starting with LingQ,
there were some issues and I ended

00:03:26.295 --> 00:03:30.645
up with some negative numbers,
but that gives you a brief sense

00:03:30.645 --> 00:03:32.955
of how long I've been at Russian.

00:03:33.015 --> 00:03:36.345
It has been a major love of mine.

00:03:36.675 --> 00:03:39.435
And so I want to get into
some detail about that.

00:03:42.015 --> 00:03:52.075
All right now, the first, um, audio
book that I listened to was The Kreutzer

00:03:52.105 --> 00:03:55.425
Sonata by Tolstoy because it's short.

00:03:55.815 --> 00:03:58.575
And in fact I have it here.

00:04:00.105 --> 00:04:00.645
Okay.

00:04:01.125 --> 00:04:02.835
And I listened to it.

00:04:02.835 --> 00:04:05.645
I didn't really understand
it, but I was able to import

00:04:06.330 --> 00:04:10.920
the, uh, text because it's all,
you know, free of copyright.

00:04:11.070 --> 00:04:13.370
I worked on it at LingQ.

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:19.110
I did Chekhov, I did a lot
of the classics of Russian.

00:04:19.410 --> 00:04:24.659
I can remember being in a place called
Silver Star where in fact, I was training

00:04:24.659 --> 00:04:29.909
to ski in the Vasaloppet in Sweden,
and I was out there cross country

00:04:29.909 --> 00:04:34.970
skiing three, four, five hours a day
while listening to Chekhov, listening

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:41.310
to Tolstoy, listening to, um, other
classics of, of Russian literature.

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:43.560
Go back to my room and look up words.

00:04:43.930 --> 00:04:45.120
LingQ was very slow.

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:48.630
Three, four, five seconds to save
each word, but I was determined.

00:04:49.050 --> 00:04:49.380
Okay.

00:04:49.380 --> 00:04:51.060
So that's kind of where it began.

00:04:51.510 --> 00:04:58.290
Uh, some of my favorite, um, you
know, DVDs it, for example...

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:04.080
uh, although my all-time favorite
was ..., which to me was the

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:06.590
ultimate exotic Russian experience.

00:05:06.840 --> 00:05:07.150
Um...

00:05:07.150 --> 00:05:12.945
19th century, uh, some guy
from Georgia and so forth, but

00:05:12.975 --> 00:05:13.905
there's a whole bunch of them.

00:05:13.935 --> 00:05:15.105
This is another great one.

00:05:16.275 --> 00:05:17.275
You know, this was...

00:05:18.215 --> 00:05:21.885
which is a classic in Russian, in Russia.

00:05:21.885 --> 00:05:24.135
They watch it uh,
apparently every new years.

00:05:24.675 --> 00:05:29.985
Uh, I got into some of the, uh,
some of the more modern ones, but

00:05:29.985 --> 00:05:31.665
I find that the modern Russian...

00:05:32.044 --> 00:05:32.775
there's also...

00:05:33.174 --> 00:05:33.934
somewhere.

00:05:34.335 --> 00:05:35.985
Uh, here we have.

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:40.470
But, um, the modern Russian
movies are a little more violent.

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:47.460
The old Russian Soviet movies,
you know, uh, here we have

00:05:47.460 --> 00:05:49.740
another one, these were great.

00:05:49.770 --> 00:05:53.260
They had a, uh, gotta get my glasses on...

00:05:53.290 --> 00:05:54.000
whatever.

00:05:54.840 --> 00:05:57.390
And so that was that.

00:05:57.540 --> 00:06:00.990
However, another major, and
I'll give you a bit of a tour

00:06:00.990 --> 00:06:03.420
now, then maybe of my library.

00:06:03.945 --> 00:06:08.805
Yeah, I did want to give you a little bit
of an overview of some of the material

00:06:08.805 --> 00:06:12.705
that I have used for my Russian here.

00:06:12.705 --> 00:06:13.555
For example...

00:06:15.234 --> 00:06:16.695
was one of my favorites.

00:06:16.725 --> 00:06:19.005
I listened to it over and over again.

00:06:19.725 --> 00:06:26.055
Uh, the same is true of Yama by Kuprin,
but these of course were later on.

00:06:26.565 --> 00:06:27.795
The very first...

00:06:27.795 --> 00:06:31.125
let me see, the very first
audio book that I did was

00:06:31.830 --> 00:06:34.030
um, The Kreutzer Sonata...

00:06:35.150 --> 00:06:36.110
by Tolstoy.

00:06:36.659 --> 00:06:40.920
I did the whole Assimil series, but of
course, as I'll show you, when I get

00:06:40.920 --> 00:06:46.800
to the books, that was only one of the,
um, learning materials that I used.

00:06:47.310 --> 00:06:50.820
Uh, of course I watched
a lot of Russian videos.

00:06:50.820 --> 00:06:55.469
I went and got some of these DVDs
just to display them here for you.

00:06:56.039 --> 00:06:57.900
Uh, one of my favorites was...

00:07:00.035 --> 00:07:00.625
You know...

00:07:01.435 --> 00:07:05.655
uh, I mean, I could go on and on.

00:07:05.715 --> 00:07:11.055
My favorite was ... which I wasn't
able to find in a hurry here.

00:07:11.265 --> 00:07:14.265
These are some of the
CDs that I listened to.

00:07:14.745 --> 00:07:19.065
Um, you know, and I have only displayed
some of them because I've got a

00:07:19.065 --> 00:07:23.175
whole bookshelf full of these CDs.

00:07:23.415 --> 00:07:28.065
So that's just the sort
of DVD, CD, uh, section.

00:07:28.215 --> 00:07:31.395
When I say listening and
reading, I include reading.

00:07:31.425 --> 00:07:37.545
So I have a lot of books here in Russian,
including books for learning Russian

00:07:37.635 --> 00:07:40.215
and books for me to read in Russian.

00:07:40.995 --> 00:07:45.225
And, uh, I'm sure I have
others elsewhere in my library.

00:07:45.605 --> 00:07:47.325
Of course it's a bit messy here, we're

00:07:47.940 --> 00:07:49.020
doing work in the house.

00:07:49.020 --> 00:07:53.909
This is my Korean section, my
German section, and so forth.

00:07:54.090 --> 00:07:59.960
At least of my, uh, CDs because the
DVDs, the movies are more to, to

00:08:00.020 --> 00:08:02.599
encourage and stimulate and motivate me.

00:08:02.810 --> 00:08:07.580
And it was fun, but the learning was
from listening to the audio books and

00:08:07.580 --> 00:08:11.330
then reading them on LingQ and saving
words and phrases and accumulating

00:08:11.330 --> 00:08:13.700
this total of 90,000 some odd

00:08:14.289 --> 00:08:15.450
known words.

00:08:15.825 --> 00:08:20.385
Granted in, you know, the way there are
so many different forms of the same word.

00:08:20.385 --> 00:08:24.795
You can't just start beating your chest
and saying, I know 90,000 words, but it

00:08:24.795 --> 00:08:30.075
indicates that I've done a lot of reading
and where I've read I've largely listened.

00:08:31.245 --> 00:08:34.395
But a major impact on my Russian learning

00:08:34.875 --> 00:08:38.385
uh, aside from the tremendous help I
got from Yevgeny and other tutors, there

00:08:38.385 --> 00:08:42.450
was a while I should have shown that in
my statistics, there was a while where

00:08:42.450 --> 00:08:45.240
I was doing a lot of talking as well,
because I mean, we're talking about a

00:08:45.240 --> 00:08:47.580
long period of engagement with Russian.

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:49.620
So perhaps two years I didn't speak.

00:08:49.770 --> 00:08:54.240
But then for another couple of years,
I'd be on talking to Alana to, uh,

00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:58.530
to Yevgeny other tutors, a number
of tutors that I had, LingQ tutors,

00:08:59.430 --> 00:09:02.580
and, and speaking and learning
about Russia and learning Russian.

00:09:02.580 --> 00:09:04.680
And it was just a wonderful,
wonderful experience.

00:09:05.355 --> 00:09:09.135
And, um, but w w a major
influence Ekho Moskvy.

00:09:09.425 --> 00:09:12.465
Now, Ekho Muskvy is a
radio station in Moscow.

00:09:12.855 --> 00:09:17.365
Not very popular in Russia
because it's somewhat anti-regime.

00:09:17.385 --> 00:09:20.835
Although in fact, there's quite a wide
range of opinion there, they have, like,

00:09:20.975 --> 00:09:24.015
you know, ... the communist is there.

00:09:24.015 --> 00:09:26.275
You've got, uh, Um...

00:09:26.325 --> 00:09:27.675
I can't remember.

00:09:27.915 --> 00:09:30.525
It's been awhile, I haven't been
listening to it for a while, but you had

00:09:30.525 --> 00:09:36.735
there's this imperialist guy and they
have a sort of a wide range of opinion.

00:09:37.785 --> 00:09:39.795
I wouldn't call them moderate in any way.

00:09:39.795 --> 00:09:45.245
They're all very emphatic and let they
have to say, but if you listen to a, a

00:09:45.275 --> 00:09:50.115
group of these people, you get a sense of
what people are talking about in Russia.

00:09:50.295 --> 00:09:54.795
The big advantage of Ekho Moskvy is
that every day there are, I don't know,

00:09:54.795 --> 00:09:58.305
a dozen interviews, audio with text.

00:09:58.395 --> 00:10:03.385
So every day I would
import the text into LingQ.

00:10:03.625 --> 00:10:08.194
I wouldn't  bother imoarting the audio
because I would have that on my, uh,

00:10:08.255 --> 00:10:12.965
you know, iPhone or whatever, but I'D go
through and mine it for words and phrases.

00:10:13.204 --> 00:10:14.525
I mean, I did that for years.

00:10:14.585 --> 00:10:17.405
Every day I would load
up with five interviews.

00:10:17.405 --> 00:10:20.555
I'd be listening in the car,
listening everywhere, reading.

00:10:21.114 --> 00:10:27.495
Ekho Moskvy was a major influence
on me developing my Russian.

00:10:28.165 --> 00:10:31.335
Now, Russians get on and say,
ah, that's a, you know, they're

00:10:31.335 --> 00:10:35.775
all, uh, American Stooges and
blah, blah, blah, and whatever.

00:10:35.775 --> 00:10:36.255
But the...

00:10:36.255 --> 00:10:38.055
in fact, it's, that's not quite fair.

00:10:38.055 --> 00:10:43.155
There is a variety, but as with any,
uh, information source, you have

00:10:43.155 --> 00:10:46.485
to pick and choose, and you can't
just accept things at face value.

00:10:46.485 --> 00:10:50.300
And I don't accept everything at face
value that I hear on Ekho Moskvy.

00:10:51.480 --> 00:10:52.890
However, it helped me a great deal.

00:10:54.340 --> 00:10:58.910
So basically my experience with
Russian was one of discovering.

00:10:58.940 --> 00:11:02.310
First of all, the classics,
because there I was able to...

00:11:02.400 --> 00:11:09.090
I went to Riga, Latvia to buy all
of these, all of these, uh, CDs.

00:11:09.090 --> 00:11:11.610
I got lots of them, which you
don't need to do now because

00:11:11.610 --> 00:11:12.689
you can find them online.

00:11:13.530 --> 00:11:17.459
Uh, and I bought all these DVDs
movies, which you don't need to do

00:11:17.459 --> 00:11:20.790
now because you can find them in
Netflix, you can find them in YouTube.

00:11:21.449 --> 00:11:26.910
Um, and, and so I was exploring that
aspect of Russia, but at the same time

00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:31.949
through Ekho Moskvy, I was exploring
daily events, Russian perspectives

00:11:31.949 --> 00:11:33.630
on things happening around the world,

00:11:34.020 --> 00:11:40.530
um, Russia?US Relations, Russia/ukraine
relations, Russia/china relations.

00:11:40.920 --> 00:11:43.410
And so I was getting a rather broad base.

00:11:43.650 --> 00:11:48.209
The other thing that Russian did for me
is it got me into the Slavic languages.

00:11:48.240 --> 00:11:49.920
It was my first Slavic language.

00:11:50.520 --> 00:11:52.680
And of course, yeah, it is difficult.

00:11:53.939 --> 00:11:56.849
It is difficult because there
are many things in Russian

00:11:56.849 --> 00:11:57.990
that we don't have in English.

00:11:58.020 --> 00:12:01.460
We don't have, uh, you
know, verbs of motion.

00:12:01.530 --> 00:12:02.790
We don't have cases.

00:12:02.790 --> 00:12:09.440
We don't have, uh, uh, you know,
aspects of verbs, uh, these things.

00:12:09.510 --> 00:12:13.340
And you can read the explanations
over and over again, but, and they're

00:12:13.340 --> 00:12:17.330
kind of helpful, but the explanations
are not that difficult to understand,

00:12:17.630 --> 00:12:20.570
except that there are so many
exceptions that you can get confused.

00:12:20.720 --> 00:12:26.210
But it's getting, developing these habits,
these patterns of speech that they have

00:12:26.210 --> 00:12:27.770
in Russian, which are different from

00:12:28.110 --> 00:12:30.750
english, and that just
takes a lot of exposure.

00:12:30.900 --> 00:12:35.160
And even at that, as I've been
away from Russian, and of course my

00:12:35.160 --> 00:12:40.050
Russian, uh, deteriorates, I make more
grammatical mistakes, I search for words.

00:12:40.260 --> 00:12:44.520
But again, if I get back
into it, if I'm with Russian

00:12:44.520 --> 00:12:46.260
people, I hear how they speak.

00:12:46.260 --> 00:12:47.880
I pick up on how they speak.

00:12:48.330 --> 00:12:51.870
I start to refresh, you
know, the habits that I have,

00:12:52.635 --> 00:12:56.444
you know, lost and acquire
new and better habits.

00:12:56.475 --> 00:12:59.235
So I'm comfortable with
where I am in Russian.

00:12:59.415 --> 00:13:04.515
But Russian is, uh, because I don't have
to write exams in Russian I just have

00:13:04.515 --> 00:13:09.495
to enjoy the language, but it was also
the door into other Slavic languages.

00:13:09.765 --> 00:13:15.015
So, uh, after Russian I learned Czech
because my father, my parents were born

00:13:15.344 --> 00:13:19.605
in, uh, actually the Austro-Hungarian
empire, but what became Czechoslovakia.

00:13:20.040 --> 00:13:24.900
And, uh, my father had, you know,
scrapbooks at home of where he climbed

00:13:24.900 --> 00:13:27.780
mountains and did a whole bunch of
stuff that was in the newspapers.

00:13:27.780 --> 00:13:28.680
And now I'm able to read it.

00:13:28.680 --> 00:13:29.410
So I learned Czech.

00:13:29.729 --> 00:13:31.410
Czech was a long road, by the way.

00:13:31.949 --> 00:13:37.949
And then from Czech, I did Polish and
then I did Ukrainian and a little bit of

00:13:37.949 --> 00:13:39.900
a Slovak, which is very similar to Czech.

00:13:40.079 --> 00:13:43.170
So it opened up the whole Slavic world.

00:13:43.859 --> 00:13:47.520
Uh, so it's been to me, it's
been a love affair with Russian.

00:13:47.970 --> 00:13:52.540
Uh, a voyage of discovery of Russian,
uh, of the ... of the Russian

00:13:52.560 --> 00:13:54.540
world, but also the Slavic world.

00:13:54.930 --> 00:13:57.210
So, uh, just a quick summary.

00:13:57.210 --> 00:14:00.600
I hope that's helpful to you
of how I learned Russian.

00:14:01.020 --> 00:14:01.620
Bye for now.

